Tasks: dalogin connection pool implementation analysis (Agroal vs HikariCP)

Phase 1 — Current state (`@backend-dev`)
- [x] Confirm Agroal is supplied through `quarkus-jdbc-mysql` in `dalogin-quarkus/pom.xml`.
- [x] Record the current pool configuration values.
- [x] Confirm no HikariCP dependency exists in the module today.
- [x] Record pool settings used by `mbook-quarkus` and `mbooks-quarkus` for comparison.
- [ ] Check the MySQL server-side maximum connection limit.

Phase 2 — Integration comparison (`@backend-dev`)
- [ ] Document Agroal configuration surface and key naming.
- [ ] Document Agroal transaction enlistment under Narayana JTA.
- [ ] Document Agroal health check and metrics integration.
- [ ] Document Agroal dev-services and dev-mode behavior.
- [ ] Determine whether a supported Quarkus HikariCP extension exists.
- [ ] Document what a manual HikariCP `DataSource` producer would require.
- [ ] List Quarkus features lost or hand-rolled under HikariCP.
- [ ] Compare native-image and build-time implications.
- [ ] Metrics exposure through Micrometer/Prometheus: skipped for now (needs minikube); revisit
      with the later performance test.

Phase 3 — Problem validation (`@backend-dev`)
- [ ] Search local dalogin logs for pool exhaustion or acquisition-timeout events (Grafana skipped
      with metrics).
- [ ] Check for connection leak symptoms in dalogin.
- [ ] Identify any latency issue attributable to connection acquisition.
- [ ] Record explicitly whether a real pooling defect exists; if none, a migration is justified only
      as a one-to-one drop-in with no compile-level impact.

Phase 4 — Measurement (`@performance-engineer`)
- [ ] Confirm the combined persistence/Servlet adaptation pass has landed before measuring, so the
      fallback path cannot create unpooled connections.
- [ ] Confirm the local topology: homebrew Apache HTTPS reverse proxy in front of a running
      `dalogin-quarkus`; `apachectl configtest` passes.
- [ ] Check the `login_` database for the users `API2.jmx` expects; seed if missing.
- [ ] Run `API2.jmx` against dalogin through the Apache HTTPS endpoint with current pool settings.
- [ ] Record acquisition latency, active count, and idle count.
- [ ] Record any timeout or exhaustion events.
- [ ] Repeat with a deliberately small pool to characterize exhaustion.
- [ ] Repeat the primary run to confirm measurement stability.

Phase 5 — Tuning recommendation (`@performance-engineer` + `@backend-dev`)
- [ ] Recommend `min-size` with rationale.
- [ ] Recommend `max-size` with rationale.
- [ ] Recommend `acquisition-timeout` with rationale.
- [ ] Cross-check the combined pool sizes of all services against the MySQL limit.
- [ ] Verify the recommendation against `k8infra/quarkus-backend.yaml`.

Phase 6 — Decision (`@backend-dev`)
- [ ] State the keep-or-migrate recommendation.
- [ ] Tie the recommendation to measured evidence, not preference.
- [ ] If keeping Agroal, record HikariCP as evaluated and declined with reasons.
- [ ] If recommending HikariCP, specify the required follow-up implementation plan.

Phase 7 — Closure (`@testing-engineer` + `@backend-dev`)
- [ ] Verify each comparison claim cites configuration, source, or measurement.
- [ ] Apply the tuning recommendation to `application.properties` if approved.
- [ ] Re-run the flow suite after any configuration change.
- [ ] Update `.ai/memory/k8s.yaml` if durable pooling topology facts changed.
- [ ] Update `speckit.system.refactoring-mentions-roadmap.plan` inventory item 2 with the decision.
